  ],
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every finite index type iota, let E = EuclideanSpace(R, iota) with its standard Borel structure. For all measures mu, pi, nu on E and gamma on E x E, put ell(x) = Real.log((mu.rnDeriv pi x).toReal), using the library-selected Radon-Nikodym representative, and s(x) = gradient ell x, the Riesz inverse of the real Frechet derivative. For a measure eta on E x E, let C(eta) be the extended-nonnegative integral of ENNReal.ofReal(||x-y||^2), and let K(mu,nu) be the infimum of C(eta) over all measures eta whose coordinate pushforwards are mu and nu. Suppose gamma has these coordinate pushforwards and C(gamma) = K(mu,nu); mu is absolutely continuous with respect to pi; ell is differentiable at mu-almost every point; x -> ||s(x)||^2 is integrable under mu; and x -> ||x||^2 is integrable under both mu and nu. Then |integral_(E x E) <s(x), y-x> dgamma(x,y)| <= sqrt(integral_E ||s(x)||^2 dmu(x)) * (K(mu,nu)^(1/2)).toReal. The integrals outside the definition of C are real Bochner integrals, the displayed sqrt is the real square root, and K^(1/2) is the extended-nonnegative square root. Real.log, ENNReal.toReal, and the gradient are total definitions: Real.log(0) = 0, ENNReal.toReal(+infinity) = 0, and the gradient is zero where ell is not differentiable. No probability normalization or finite-total-mass assumption is imposed.",
  "reconstructed_text_sha256": "03c1c7a1da50665ed34c73d935b7352728c188876170680d0a3389b913c78b42",
  "objects": "A finite index type iota; the real Euclidean space E indexed by iota; measures mu, pi, nu on E and gamma on E x E; the library-selected RN log ratio ell of mu relative to pi; its total gradient s; quadratic transport costs C; their coupling infimum K; information I = integral_E ||s||^2 dmu; and extended distance W = K^(1/2).",
  "domains": "iota : Type u_1 with Fintype iota; E = EuclideanSpace R iota with its standard Borel structure. mu, pi, nu : Measure E and gamma : Measure (E x E). ell : E -> R and s : E -> E. C, K, W take values in the extended nonnegative reals; I, the pairing integral, the real square root of I, and W.toReal are real-valued.",
  "quantifiers": "Universally quantified over every finite index type iota, every mu, pi, nu, every gamma, and every proof of the four displayed hypotheses hgamma, hscore, hmu, hnu. The differentiability condition in hscore holds mu-almost everywhere. The infimum defining K ranges over all couplings of the specified mu and nu.",
  "assumptions": "hgamma: gamma has first coordinate pushforward mu and second coordinate pushforward nu, and C(gamma) equals K(mu,nu). hscore: mu is absolutely continuous with respect to pi, ell is differentiable at mu-almost every point, and ||s||^2 is integrable under mu. hmu: ||x||^2 is integrable under mu. hnu: ||x||^2 is integrable under nu.",
  "conclusion": "The absolute value of the real integral of <s(x), y-x> under gamma is at most sqrt(I) times W.toReal, where I is precisely information mu pi hscore and W is precisely the extended-nonnegative quadratic Wasserstein distance defined by the coupling-cost infimum.",
  "scopes": "The same mu and pi determine ell, s, the score-domain condition, and information; gamma transports mu to nu. Squared-score integrability and differentiability are imposed only under mu; second-moment integrability is imposed under mu and nu. pi has no separate moment or normalization condition. No measure is assumed to be a probability measure or to have finite total mass, and no pointwise global differentiability or smooth density hypothesis is added. The RN representative is fixed by the library. Real.log(0) = 0 and ENNReal.toReal(+infinity) = 0; the total gradient is zero at points of nondifferentiability. The conversion toReal is applied to the extended square root of K, not inserted into its cost integrals.",
  "constant_dependencies": "The coefficient is exactly 1. There are no additional dimension-dependent, measure-dependent, regularity-dependent, or unspecified constants. The exponents 2 and 1/2 belong to the displayed squared norms and transport square root. hscore is a proof argument certifying the domain of information, whose value is the stated squared-gradient integral.",
